bodyEn:
Cambodia was a French protectorate from 1863 to 1953. During this time, Khmer boxing changed from a rough traditional contest into a sport with clear rules.

Historians and the English Wikipedia describe the French colonial administration codifying the sport in the early 20th century. The new rule set mixed ideas from Western boxing with the Khmer fighting tradition. The main changes reported are:

- a roped boxing ring instead of an open ground;
- Western-style boxing gloves;
- rounds of fixed length, timed by a clock;
- a written set of rules and officials to enforce them.

Why did this matter? Reports say that before these reforms, fights were very dangerous, and serious injuries or deaths in the ring were not unusual in the 1920s and 1930s. Gloves, timed rounds and referees made bouts safer and easier to organise as public events.

Many features fans still recognise today, such as the ring and the round structure, date from this period, while the Kun Kru ritual and live music kept the sport's Khmer character.
